course: dictionary
lesson: 7775
title: Putty: one word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Putty is a good example. It can be a soft paste for filling gaps, a squishy toy, a way of describing someone who is easily led, or even a verb for filling something. The sentence around the word shows you which meaning to use.
explain: Look at this line from a science book: «The mantle is about 2,900 km thick and is solid rock, but over millions of years it slowly flows like very stiff putty.» The word "flows" and the phrase "very stiff" tell you putty here means a material that can slowly change shape. It is not about a window or a toy.
explain: Now try a story: «He was putty in her hands, and she got him to agree to anything.» Putty in someone's hands is an idiom. It means the person is easily persuaded or controlled. Read the whole sentence to check: "agree to anything" shows he was easily won over.
fact: Putty is a soft paste, often grey or white, that is pressed into gaps and goes hard as it dries.
fact: «Putty» can also mean any soft stuff that is easy to press and shape, like «silly putty».
fact: The idiom «putty in someone's hands» means a person who is easily persuaded or controlled.
fact: As a verb, «to putty» means to fill or fix something with putty, but it is rare.
fact: Context clues, such as «flows» in a science text or «agree to anything» in a story, help you pick the right meaning.
exercise: «The window glass was loose, so Dad pressed fresh putty around the frame.» What is putty in this sentence? | A sweet cake with cream | A kind of bird that lives near water | A loud noise made by a door | *A soft paste that goes hard as it dries
exercise: Ella says «silly putty» is just a sticky mess. Which meaning of putty does «silly putty» use? | A word for someone who is easily persuaded | *Any soft stuff that can be pressed and shaped easily | A tool for scraping paint | A hard paste for filling holes in walls
exercise: «When she smiled, the strict guard was putty in her hands.» What does this tell you about the guard? | *He was easily won over by her | He was made of clay | He was very strong and tall | He had lost his hands
test: «The putty knife scraped the old paint off the window.» What is a putty knife used for? | *Spreading and scraping putty | Playing a tune on the piano | Measuring the length of a room | Cutting bread at dinner
test: «He puttied the cracks in the wall before painting it.» What does puttied mean here? | Painted in bright colours | *Filled with putty | Cleaned with water and soap | Broke into small pieces
exam: «The mantle ... flows like very stiff putty.» What does this sentence tell you about the mantle? | *Rock can flow slowly over millions of years, like a very thick, soft material | Rock is always runny like water | Rock floats on top of the sea | Rock can slowly change shape over millions of years
